Introduction
An Expert Advisor opens a BUY on EURUSD at 12:35 on the M5 chart and reads an H1 trend value. Was the value available at 12:35, or does it reflect the H1 candle's final close at 13:00? An equity curve cannot answer that question.
A forming candle is not automatically evidence of look-ahead: an Expert Advisor may use its current value under an intrabar policy. The key is to establish which value was used, when it became available, and whether that timing is consistent with the decision policy.
This article builds an MQL5 library to make those assumptions explicit. You will learn how to record the inputs behind a decision, select a closed-bar or intrabar policy, and reproduce named validation failures with deterministic scenarios. The audit checks declared timing only. It cannot detect an input that was not recorded, or prove that an indicator did not repaint.
When a Backtest Knows Too Much
Look-ahead leaks rarely come from deliberate intent. They come from assumptions that hold in one context and break in another:
Shift 0 in closed-bar logic.
Index 0 is the currently forming bar, and its value keeps moving until the bar closes.
Higher-timeframe reads.
The current H1 value keeps changing until 13:00, so a 12:35 decision that ends up with its final version has consumed 25 minutes of future information.
Other symbols.
GBPUSD and EURUSD tick independently, so a GBPUSD value can carry a timestamp later than the EURUSD decision that uses it.
Index arithmetic.
A negative shift or an off-by-one offset can point at a bar that has not started yet.
The H1 case is the reference example for the rest of the article. At 12:35, the previous H1 bar is complete, while the current one is only partly formed, as shown below.
Figure 1. An M5 decision at 12:35 against the closed H1 bar (11:00–12:00) and the forming H1 bar (12:00–13:00)
An equity curve cannot separate these cases, because it never shows which version of a bar the logic read. The attached demo Expert Advisor makes the difference visible. It runs five scenarios anchored to 2026.01.01 12:35: the two correctly built ones pass, while the future bar, the unfinished H1 candle, and the mismatched timestamp fail.
Figure 2. Experiment summary printed by TemporalAuditDemo.mq5 in the Experts log
Implementation in MQL5
The implementation starts with the timing model that every check relies on and runs the original M5/H1 case as a minimal example. It then covers the validation rules, the library structure and integration workflow, and the reproducible demo and tests.
Timing Model and Validation Boundaries
Most rules compare times, so the timing model comes first. A decision involves several times, and only some of them are recorded:
Decision time
is the moment the logic commits to an action, such as 12:35.
Bar open and close times
delimit the bar a value belongs to. A bar that closes after the decision was still forming when the decision was made.
Observation timestamp
is the time at which the value became available, as asserted by the caller. For a closed bar, the convention is the bar's close time. For a forming bar, it is the moment the value was sampled.
Read time and indicator calculation time
describe when the Expert Advisor retrieved the value and when the indicator computed it. Neither is recorded.
Because the observation timestamp describes availability, a value may be read long after it became available and still pass. A value from the 09:55–10:00 bar read at 10:05 for a 10:10 decision is declared with an observation timestamp of 10:00. Recording 10:05 instead would put a read time into the availability field, and Rule 5 would reject the declaration.
Bar boundaries and the closed flag come from the caller and are taken as given. Reading them back with iTime() would tie each verdict to the loaded history and rule out tests without market data. Some history-free checks would remain deterministic, for example comparing the bar length with PeriodSeconds() of its timeframe. The prototype does not implement these checks yet. Its verdicts are therefore only as reliable as the caller's metadata.
Each verdict pairs a status (TEMPORAL_PASS, TEMPORAL_WARNING, or TEMPORAL_FAIL) with a violation type. Every violation type other than VIOLATION_NONE maps to exactly one rule; VIOLATION_NONE is used for PASS and WARNING outcomes.
// --- Specific violation types detected by the validator
enum
ENUM_VIOLATION_TYPE
  {
   VIOLATION_NONE,
// No violation detected
VIOLATION_FUTURE_TIMESTAMP,
// Observation timestamp after the decision time
VIOLATION_FUTURE_BAR,
// Referenced bar opens after the decision time
VIOLATION_UNFINISHED_BAR,
// Unfinished bar used in closed-bar mode
VIOLATION_MTF_TIMING,
// Higher-timeframe candle not closed at decision time
VIOLATION_TIMESTAMP_MISMATCH,
// Observation timestamp outside its own bar range
VIOLATION_INVALID_SHIFT,
// Bar shift is negative (only negative shifts are caught)
VIOLATION_CROSS_SYMBOL_TIMING
// Cross-symbol dependency with a future observation timestamp
};
The STemporalAuditResult structure stores both fields together with the decision context and all dependency metadata, so each result can explain itself.
A Minimal Closed-Bar M5/H1 Example
Before the rules are examined one by one, the reference case from Figure 1 can be run end to end. The demo Expert Advisor sets the closed-bar policy in OnInit(). These values match the constructor defaults, but setting them explicitly keeps the policy visible at the call site.
