The complete Validate() method is in the attached CTemporalValidator.mqh. Each rule there is a short if block that sets the status, the violation type, and a diagnostic message, and then returns. The method relies on three private helpers.
//--- IsHigherTimeframe - compare two timeframes by actual duration
bool
IsHigherTimeframe(
const
ENUM_TIMEFRAMES
a,
const
ENUM_TIMEFRAMES
b)
const
{
int
seconds_a =
PeriodSeconds
(a);
// period duration of the first timeframe
int
seconds_b =
PeriodSeconds
(b);
// period duration of the second timeframe
return
(seconds_a > seconds_b);
// true when the first is strictly longer
}
//--- IsInsideBarRange - check whether a timestamp lies inside a bar range
bool
IsInsideBarRange(
const
datetime
ts,
const
datetime
bar_open,
const
datetime
bar_close)
const
{
return
(ts >= bar_open && ts <= bar_close);
// inclusive range comparison
}
//--- DECISION_ON_BAR_CLOSE always requires closed bars. The
//--- require_closed_bars flag enforces closed bars independently,
//--- which allows a stricter configuration than the decision mode
//--- alone would imply.
bool
RequiresClosedBars()
const
{
return
(m_config.decision_mode == DECISION_ON_BAR_CLOSE) || m_config.require_closed_bars;
// combined policy check
}
Rules 1–4 reject inputs that cannot be trusted at decision time. A bar opening after the decision fails Rule 1 even when its shift is also negative. Rule 2 rejects negative shifts, but it cannot check large positive ones without price history. Rule 3 catches a cross-symbol timestamp later than the decision. It compares without tolerance by design, treating a later quote from another symbol as a synchronization failure. Stale quotes and other synchronization problems are not detected. Rule 4 rejects a higher-timeframe bar that closes after the decision, which is exactly the H1 case from Figure 1. IsHigherTimeframe() compares durations, not the numeric order of ENUM_TIMEFRAMES values.
Rules 5–8 cover consistency, policy, and precision. Under the availability convention, a value cannot become available outside its own bar, so Rule 5 rejects an observation timestamp outside the bar range. The range is inclusive because a closed bar's value becomes available exactly at its close; the cost is that a boundary timestamp also equals the next bar's open. Rule 6 enforces the closed-bar policy, and Rules 7 and 8 apply the tolerance.
Because the validator returns on the first match, rule order is a reporting policy, not a severity ranking. Only the first failing rule is reported; it may not be the root cause. Two cases from the attached code illustrate this:
A bar opening at 12:40 with shift −1 is reported as FUTURE_BAR rather than INVALID_SHIFT.
A forming H1 bar in closed-bar mode is reported as MTF_TIMING rather than UNFINISHED_BAR.
Disabling Rule 3 or Rule 4 in the configuration lets the dependency fall through to the next rule that applies.
Rules 7 and 8 compare the observation timestamp with the decision time as follows:
If t_obs ≤ t_dec, the dependency passes.
If t_dec < t_obs ≤ t_dec + τ, the result is WARNING.
If t_obs > t_dec + τ, the result is FAIL with VIOLATION_FUTURE_TIMESTAMP.
The tolerance absorbs rounding between time sources, such as a tick time and a bar time. It applies to Rules 7 and 8 only; Rules 1, 3, 4, and 5 compare times exactly. A WARNING is not a softer pass. It means the recorded metadata is too coarse to decide either way. With the default τ of 1 second, the warning zone covers a single datetime step.
RequiresClosedBars() returns true in closed-bar mode or whenever require_closed_bars is set, so the flag can only tighten the policy. A forming bar can therefore pass only under DECISION_INTRABAR with the flag cleared. Even then, a forming bar from a higher timeframe is rejected by Rule 4 as long as reject_unfinished_mtf is true. The five policy fields and their defaults are summarized below.
