Audit() then passes every record to the validator.
//--- Audit - validate every recorded dependency against the context
int
Audit()
     {
int
count = m_tracker.GetDependencyCount();
// number of recorded dependencies
ArrayResize
(m_results, count);
// allocate result slots
datetime
decision_time = m_tracker.GetDecisionTime();
// decision timestamp
string
decision_symbol = m_tracker.GetDecisionSymbol();
// decision symbol
ENUM_TIMEFRAMES
decision_timeframe = m_tracker.GetDecisionTimeframe();
// decision timeframe
//--- delegate each dependency to the validator with full context
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < count; i++)
// iterate over dependencies
{
         CTemporalDependency dep;
// dependency buffer
if
(m_tracker.GetDependency(i, dep))
// retrieve the dependency
m_validator.Validate(dep, decision_time, decision_symbol, decision_timeframe, m_results[i]);
// validate
else
{
            m_results[i].status = TEMPORAL_WARNING;
// fallback status on retrieval failure
m_results[i].violation = VIOLATION_NONE;
// no violation recorded
m_results[i].message =
"Dependency retrieval failed."
;
// diagnostic message
}
        }
return
count;
// return audited count
}
If a record cannot be retrieved, its slot becomes a WARNING, so an unreadable dependency never passes silently. The method returns the number of dependencies audited, and GetOverallStatus() folds the results into one verdict.
//--- GetOverallStatus - aggregate status across all dependencies
ENUM_TEMPORAL_STATUS GetOverallStatus()
const
{
bool
has_warning =
false
;
// flag for warning presence
for
(
int
i =
0
; i <
ArraySize
(m_results); i++)
// iterate over all results
{
if
(m_results[i].status == TEMPORAL_FAIL)
// fail short-circuits
return
TEMPORAL_FAIL;
// return fail immediately
if
(m_results[i].status == TEMPORAL_WARNING)
// warning detected
has_warning =
true
;
// remember warning presence
}
if
(has_warning)
// any warnings found
return
TEMPORAL_WARNING;
// return warning status
return
TEMPORAL_PASS;
// otherwise all pass
}
Any FAIL wins, then any WARNING, and otherwise the result is PASS. An empty result array also yields PASS, so a caller should confirm that Audit() returned a positive count before trusting the verdict. CCausalityReport only formats the results for the Experts log and contains no rules.
To reproduce the results, install and run the attached files in the following order:
Copy the seven include files to MQL5\Include\TemporalAudit, TemporalAuditDemo.mq5 to MQL5\Experts\TemporalAudit, and TemporalAuditTest.mq5 to MQL5\Scripts\TemporalAudit. Both programs include the headers as <TemporalAudit/...>, so the folder name must match.
Compile TemporalAuditDemo.mq5 and TemporalAuditTest.mq5 in MetaEditor.
Attach TemporalAuditDemo to any chart. The demo runs in OnInit() and writes to the Experts tab. Its experiment table should show PASS for "Correct causal decision" and "Correct MTF", and FAIL for "Future bar", "Unfinished H1 candle", and "Timestamp mismatch". The supplementary intrabar block should report PASS.
Check the detailed diagnostics. Each dependency block lists the bar range with its state, the dependency and decision times, the status, and the reason set by the rule that fired. For the unfinished H1 candle, the reason reads "Higher-timeframe candle was not closed at the decision time."
Run TemporalAuditTest on any chart. Each test prints a PASS or FAIL line, and the summary should read "Total: 12   Passed: 12   Failed: 0".
