In test 09, the observation timestamp sits one second after the decision, inside the tolerance, so the test expects WARNING rather than FAIL. Test 03 needs a word of caution: its 12:35–12:40 bar is marked closed at 12:35, which is physically impossible. That flag is what lets it pass Rule 6 and reach Rule 7; with the correct flag, Rule 6 would report UNFINISHED_BAR. No rule currently checks the closed flag against the bar times.
Each outcome is checked by ExpectStatus().
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ExpectStatus - check overall status and first-dependency class   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
ExpectStatus(
const
CTemporalAudit &audit,
const
ENUM_TEMPORAL_STATUS expected_status,
const
ENUM_VIOLATION_TYPE expected_violation)
  {
//--- confirm the aggregated status is correct
if
(audit.GetOverallStatus() != expected_status)
// compare aggregated status
return
false
;
// mismatch detected
//--- confirm the first dependency carries the expected violation
STemporalAuditResult res;
// result buffer
if
(!audit.GetResult(
0
, res))
// retrieve first dependency
return
false
;
// retrieval failed
return
(res.violation == expected_violation);
// compare violation type
}
The helper compares both the overall status and the violation type of the first dependency. The second comparison matters. The GBPUSD input in test 08 breaks Rules 3 and 7, so a status-only check would still see FAIL if Rule 3 were removed or reordered. In test 10, the second dependency is covered through the overall status. VIOLATION_TIMESTAMP_MISMATCH and VIOLATION_INVALID_SHIFT have no isolated test, so the script would not catch a regression in Rule 2 or Rule 5. When the library behaves as documented, all twelve tests pass.
