Limitations
The library is a research prototype and has not been wired into a live trading system. Its limitations are as follows:
Instrumentation dependency
. Only explicitly recorded inputs are validated. An input that is consumed but never recorded is invisible to the audit.
Caller-supplied metadata
. Bar boundaries and the closed flag are taken as given. Bar existence, interval direction, duration, grid alignment, closed-flag consistency, and the true source of the value are not checked, as the off-grid intrabar bar and test 03 show.
No read time
. Only the declared availability time is recorded, so the audit cannot confirm how the Expert Advisor actually behaved.
Repainting indicators
. Recalculated or look-ahead indicator values pass whenever the declared bar looks correct.
Inclusive bar boundaries
. A timestamp at a bar's close also equals the next bar's open. A half-open interval with an exception for the close would remove this ambiguity.
Tolerance scope
. The tolerance applies only to Rules 7 and 8, so a cross-symbol timestamp just one second after the decision is reported as FAIL rather than WARNING.
Narrow cross-symbol rule
. Only future cross-symbol timestamps are caught; stale or unsynchronized quotes are not.
One violation per dependency
. Only the first failing rule is reported, and it may not be the root cause.
Negative shifts only
. Large positive shifts are not checked against the available history.
No dependency ancestry
. The parent identifier is stored but never followed, so problems inherited by derived features are not traced.
Empty audits report PASS
. A decision without results reports PASS, so the count returned by Audit() should be checked.
WARNING as uncertainty
. A warning means the metadata is too coarse to decide, not that the input is acceptable.
Above all, passing the audit shows that the declared timing is consistent with the policy, not that the Expert Advisor avoided future data. This article is educational, and any strategy should be tested on a demo account before live use.
Conclusion
The library makes the declared timing of a decision's inputs checkable through eight ordered rules. Its examples demonstrate how the selected policy and the first matching rule affect the reported result. In particular, for a 12:35 M5 decision, a completed 11:00–12:00 H1 input passes under the default policy, while the still-forming 12:00–13:00 H1 input fails.
The workflow is to start a decision with BeginDecision(), record its inputs with AddDependency(), run Audit(), and inspect both the per-dependency results and GetOverallStatus(). Check that Audit() returned a positive count before accepting an overall PASS. Treat WARNING as unresolved timing uncertainty, not approval.
The demo uses fixed timestamps to make its scenarios reproducible, and the test script contains twelve assertions. However, invalid shift and timestamp mismatch do not have isolated tests, so those rule paths are not independently covered by the test suite. Finally, a passing audit means that the recorded metadata is consistent with the selected policy—not that the Expert Advisor is free of look-ahead.
